OK,

         Thanks to all the help on this one.  It turned out to be 
Persistent Reservations by the original cluster.  Here is a good short 
explanation of what these are and how to remove them in a non-clustered 
environment:

>If a disk is used in a cluster, then you try to use it in a different
>cluster or outside a cluster, there may be a persistent SCSI reservation
>left on the disk. Clusters use reservations to try and make sure shared
>disks are only accessed by the proper node.
>
>If you don't have a cluster running, you can use
>
>         /usr/sbin/cleanPR clean
>
>to remove reservations from *all* the visible disks.
>
>                                                 - Bluejay Adametz

         I was able to easily re-layout the disk mappings (/dev/disk/dsk*) 
to match the original layout.  One thing to be aware of is you're swap file 
if you rename you're current boot disk, you'll need to change 
/etc/sysconfigtab to reflect the change.  The command is 'dfsmgr -m dsk# dsk@'

         I used advscan to find domains on my disks.  This creates my links 
in /etc/fdmns.  Once I had that I just mounted them on /mnt to make sure 
they were OK and then renamed the directory in /etc/fdmns to what I wanted 
them to be.
                 '/sbin/advfs/advscan -r dsk16 dsk17 dsk25 dsk26 dsk23'

         If you have any questions, please let me know.

         thanks,

         Paul

